{{smaller block|<poem>Fadres and Modres that ''walken'' in won
Schul loue heore children.</poem>
{{right|''Holy Rood'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 143.}}
''Walk'' humbly with thy God. {{right|Micah vi. 8.}}}}
'''6.''' To move with the gait called a walk. See ''walk'', ''n.'', 5.
{{smaller block|<poem>O, let me see thee ''walk''; thou dost not halt.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', T. of the S., ii. 1. 258.}}
<poem>He ''walks'', he leaps, he runs — is wing’d with joy.</poem>
{{right|''Cowper'', Task, i. 443.}}}}
'''7.''' To go or travel on foot: often followed by an accusative of distance: as, to ''walk'' five miles.
{{smaller block|<poem>In his slepe hym thoghte
That in a forest faste he ''welk'' to wepe.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Troilus, v. 1235.}}
<poem>But, look, the morn, in russet mantle clad,
''Walks'' o’er the dew of yon high eastward hill.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Hamlet, i. 1. 167.}}
I was constrained to ''walke'' a foote for the space of seven miles. {{right|''Coryat'', Crudities, I. 92.}}
<poem>I’ll ''walk'' aside,
And come again anon.</poem>
{{right|''Fletcher'', Wildgoose Chase, iv. 3.}}}}
'''8.''' To move, after a manner somewhat analogous to walking, as an effect of repeated oscillations and twistings produced by expansion and contraction or by the action of winds. Chimneys have been known to move in this manner.—{{smaller|'''The ghost walks.''' See ''ghost''.—'''To walk against time.''' See ''time''<sup>1</sup>.—'''To walk awry.''' See ''awry''.—'''To walk into,''' to attack. (''a'') To assault; give a beating or drubbing to. (''b'') To fall foul of verbally; give a scolding to. (''c'') To eat heartily of. [Vulgar in all senses.]}}

readings.c2s1:
¶
{{smaller block|There is little Jacob, ''walking'', as the popular phrase is, ''into'' a home-made plum-cake, at a most surprising pace.
{{right|''Dickens'', Old Curiosity Shop, lxviii.}}}}

{{smaller|'''To walk over the course''', in ''sporting'', to go over a course at a walking or slow pace: said of a horse, runner, etc., coming alone to the scratch, and having to go over the course to win; hence, figuratively, to gain an easy victory; attain one’s object without opposition. Also ''to walk over''. Compare ''walk-over''.—'''To walk Spanish.''' See ''Spanish''.—'''To walk tall.''' See ''tall''<sup>2</sup>.—'''Walk about,''' a military phrase used by British officers to sentinels, to waive the ceremony of being saluted.}}

'''II.''' ''trans.'' '''1†.''' To full, as cloth.
{{smaller block|Payment vj d., for the ''walkin'' of ilke eln [ell] of the said xix eln & a half.
{{right|''Act. Dom. Conc. A.'' 1488, p. 95. (''Jamieson.'')}}}}
'''2.''' To proceed or move through, over, or upon by walking, or as if by walking; traverse at a walk.
{{smaller block|<poem>If that same demon that hath gull’d thee thus
Should with his lion gait ''walk'' the whole world.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Hen. V., ii. 2. 122.}}
<poem>Yes—she is ours—a home-returning bark; . . .
She ''walks'' the waters like a thing of life.</poem>
{{right|''Byron'', Corsair, i. 3.}}}}
'''3.''' To cause to walk; lead, drive, or ride at a walk.
{{smaller block|I will rather trust . . . a thief to ''walk'' my ambling gelding.
{{right|''Shak.'', M. W. of W., ii. 2. 319.}}
<poem>I am much indebted to you
For dancing me off my legs, and then for ''walking'' me.</poem>
{{right|''Fletcher'', Wildgoose Chase, iii. 1.}}}}
'''4.''' To escort in a walk; take to walk.
{{smaller block|I feel the dew in my great toe; but I would put on a cut shoe, that I might be able to ''walk'' you about; I may be laid up to-morrow.
{{right|''Colman and Garrick'', Clandestine Marriage, ii.}}}}

readings.c2s2:
¶
{{smaller block|Old Pendennis . . . ''walked'' the new arrivals about the park and gardens, and showed them the carte du pays.
{{right|''Thackeray'', Pendennis, lvi.}}}}
'''5.''' To move, as a box or trunk, in a manner having some analogy to walking, partly by a rocking motion, and partly by turning the object on its resting-point in such manner that at each rocking movement an alternate point of support is employed, the last one used being always in advance of the previous one in the direction toward which the object is to be moved.—'''6.''' To send to or keep in a walk. See ''walk'', ''n.'', 8 (''b'').
{{smaller block|It is customary to send puppies out at three or four months of age to be kept by cottagers, butchers, small farmers, etc., at a weekly sum for each, which is called ''walking'' them. ''Dogs of Great Brit. and America'', p. 197.}}
{{smaller block|'''To walk one’s chalks.''' See ''chalk''.—'''To walk the chalk, to walk the chalk-mark,''' to keep straight in morals or manners: a figurative phrase, from the difficulty a drunken man has in walking upon a straight line chalked upon the floor by his comrades to test his degree of sobriety. Compare I., 5.—'''To walk the hospitals,''' to attend the medical and surgical practice of a general hospital, as a student, under one or more of the regular staff of physicians or surgeons attached to such a hospital.—'''Walking the plank.''' See ''plank''.}}

'''walk''' (wâk), ''n.'' [< ME. ''walc'', ''walk'', < AS. ''gewealc'', a rolling, moving, = MHG. ''walc'' = Icel. ''válk'', a tossing; from the verb.] '''1.''' Manner of action; course, as of life; way of living: as, a person’s ''walk'' and conversation.
{{smaller block|<poem>This is the melancholy ''walk'' he lives in,
And chooses ever to increase his sadness.</poem>
{{right|''Fletcher'', Double Marriage, iv. 3.}}
<poem>Oh for a closer ''walk'' with God!</poem>
{{right|''Cowper'', Olney Hymns, i.}}}}

readings.c2s3:
¶
'''2.''' Range or sphere of action; a department, as of art, science, or literature.

{{smaller block|There are strong minds in every ''walk'' of life, that will rise superior to the disadvantages of situation.
{{right|''A. Hamilton'', The Federalist, XXXVI.}}
She [Mrs. Cibber] made some attempts latterly in comedy, which were not, however, in any degree equal to her excellence in the opposite ''walk''.
{{right|''Life of Quin'' (reprint 1887), p. 40.}}}}

'''3.''' The act of walking for air or exercise; a stroll: as, a morning ''walk''.

{{smaller block|Make an early and long ''walk'' in goodness.
{{right|''Sir T. Browne'', Christ. Mor., i. 35.}}
<poem>
Nor ''walk'' by moon,
Or glittering starlight, without thee is sweet.
</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., iv. 655.}}
<poem>
To vent thy bosom’s swelling rise
In pensive ''walk''.
</poem>
{{right|''Burns'', The Vision, ii.}}}}

'''4.''' Manner of walking; gait; step; carriage.

{{smaller block|Catherine . . . watched Miss Thorpe’s progress down the street from the drawing-room window; admired the graceful spirit of her ''walk'', the fashionable air of her figure and dress. {{right|''Jane Austen'', Northanger Abbey, iv.}}}}

'''5.''' The slowest gait of land-animals. {{smaller|In the walk of bipeds there is always one foot on the ground; in that of quadrupeds there are always two, and a part of the time three, feet on the ground. When very slow, or with heavy draft-animals when hauling, all four feet touch the ground at once for brief intervals. In the walk of ordinary quadrupeds the limbs move in diagonal pairs, the movement of the pair not being so nearly simultaneous as in}}

