readings.c3s2:
¶
'''6.''' A piece of ground fit to walk in; a place in which one is accustomed to walk; a haunt.
{{smaller block|<poem>His ''walk''
The fiery serpent fled and noxious worm.</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', P. R., i. 311.}}}}
{{smaller block|We intend to lay ambushment in the Indian’s ''walks'', to cut off their men.
{{right|''N. Thomas'' (Appendix to New England’s Memorial, p. 430).}}}}

'''7.''' A place laid out or set apart for walking; an avenue; a promenade.
{{smaller block|I saw a very goodly ''walke'' in Mantua roofed over and supported with thirty nine faire pillars.
{{right|''Coryat'', Crudities, I. 148.}}}}
{{smaller block|Specifically—(''a'') An avenue set with trees or laid out in a grove or wood.}}
{{smaller block|Get ye all three into the box-tree; Malvolio’s coming down this ''walk''. {{right|''Shak.'', T. N., ii. 5. 19.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Up that long ''walk'' of limes I past.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', In Memoriam, lxxxvii.}}}}
{{smaller block|(''b''†) ''pl.'' Grounds; a park.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>He hath left you all his ''walks'',
His private arbours and new-planted orchards,
On this side Tiber.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', J. C., iii. 2. 252.}}}}
{{smaller block|(''c'') A path in or as in a garden or street; a sidewalk: as, a flagged ''walk;'' a plank ''walk''.}}
{{smaller block|He strayed down a ''walk'' edged with box; with apple-trees, pear-trees, and cherry-trees on one side, and a border on the other, full of all sorts of old-fashioned flowers.
{{right|''Charlotte Brontë'', Jane Eyre, xx.}}}}
{{smaller block|(''d'') In public parks and the like, a place or way for retirement: as, gentlemen’s ''walk''.}}

'''8.''' A piece of ground on which domestic animals feed or have exercise.
{{smaller block|He eats the eggs for breakfast and the chickens for dinner, goes in for fancy breeds, and runs up an ornamental ''walk'' for them. {{right|''A. Jessopp'', Arcady, i.}}}}

readings.c3s3:
{{smaller|Specifically—(''a'') A tract of some extent where sheep feed; a pasture for sheep; a sheep-walk. See ''sheep-run.''}}
{{smaller block|He had ''walk'' for a hundred sheep.
{{right|''Latimer'', 1st Sermon bef. Edw. VI., 1549.}}}}
{{smaller|(''b'') A place where puppies are kept and trained for sporting purposes.}}
{{smaller block|Preference should be given to the home rearing if properly carried out, because it has all the advantages of the ''walk'' without those disadvantages attending upon it.
{{right|''Dogs of Great Britain and America'', p. 197.}}}}
{{smaller|(''c'') A pen in which a gamecock is kept with a certain amount of liberty, but separated from other cocks, to get him in condition and disposition for fighting.}}
'''9.''' A district habitually served by a hawker or itinerant vender of any commodity.
{{smaller block|One man told me . . . that he had thoughts at one time of trying to establish himself in a cats’-meat ''walk'', and made inquiries into the nature of the calling.
{{right|''Mayhew'', London Labour and London Poor, II. 10.}}}}
'''10.''' In the London Royal Exchange, any part of the ambulatory that is specially frequented by merchants or traders to some particular country. ''Simmonds.''—'''11†.''' A district in a royal forest or park marked out for hunting purposes.
{{smaller block|I will keep . . . my shoulders for the fellow of this ''walk'' [''i. e.'', Herne, the hunter, in Windsor Park].
{{right|''Shak.'', M. W. of W., v. 5. 29.}}
They like better to hunt by stealth in another man’s ''walk''. {{right|''Burton'', Anat. of Mel., p. 571.}}}}
'''12.''' A ropewalk.—'''13†.''' In ''falconry'', a flock or wisp of snipe.—{{smaller|'''Cock of the walk.''' See ''cock''<sup>1</sup>.—'''Heel-and-toe walk''', a walk in which the heel of one foot is}}
